window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-35"]=[{"id":"q4_cont_105","q":"4:105–109","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:105–109 · Justice and the treachery accusation","source":"Cairo I:556–558","cairo":"I:556–558","source_page_first":556,"source_page_last":558,"ar":"إِنَّا أَنْزَلْنا إِلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ بِالْحَقِّ لِتَحْكُمَ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ بِما أَراكَ اللَّهُ وَلا تَكُنْ لِلْخائِنِينَ خَصِيماً (105) وَاسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (106) وَلا تُجادِلْ عَنِ الَّذِينَ يَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ خَوَّاناً أَثِيماً (107) يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ النَّاسِ وَلا يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَهُوَ مَعَهُمْ إِذْ يُبَيِّتُونَ ما لا يَرْضى مِنَ الْقَوْلِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطاً (108) ها أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ جادَلْتُمْ عَنْهُمْ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا فَمَنْ يُجادِلُ اللَّهَ عَنْهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ أَمْ مَنْ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكِيلاً (109)\nقلت: أرى، هنا عرفانية، لا علمية، فلذلك لم تتعد إلى ثلاثة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله لنبيه- عليه الصلاة والسلام- حين همَّ أن يخاصم عن طُعمَة بن أبَيرِق، وذلك أنه سرق درعًا من جاره قتادة بن النعمان، في جراب دقيق، فجعل الدقيق يسقط من خرق فيه، وخبَّأها عند يهودي، فالتمس الدرع عند طعمة، فلم توجد، وحلف ما أخذها، وما له بها علم، فتركوه واتبعوا أثر الدقيق حتى انتهى إلى منزل اليهودي فأخذوها، فقال اليهودي: دَفَعَهَا إليَّ طُعمَة، وشهد له ناس من اليهود، فقال رهط طعمة من بني ظفر: انطلقوا بنا إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فنسأله أن يجادل عن صاحبنا، وقالوا: إن لم يفعل هلك وافتضح، وبرئ اليهودي، فهمَّ رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم اعتماداً على ظاهر الأمر، ولم يكن له علم بالواقعة، فنزلت الآية:\nإِنَّا أَنْزَلْنا إِلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ بِالْحَقِّ أي: ملتبسًا بالحق لِتَحْكُمَ بما فيه من الحق بَيْنَ النَّاسِ بسبب ما أَراكَ أي: عَرَّفك اللَّهُ بالوحي، أو بالاجتهاد، ففيه دليل على إثبات القياس، وبه قال الجمهور. وفي اجتهاد الأنبياء خلاف. وَلا تَكُنْ لِلْخائِنِينَ خَصِيماً أي: عنهم للبرآء، أو لأجلِهم والذَّبَّ عنهم.\nوَاسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ مما هممت به، إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً، وفيه دليل على منع الوكالة عن الذمي، وبه قال ابن شعبان. وقال ابن عات: لعله أراد الندب. وقال مالك بن دينار: كفى بالمرء خيانة أن يكون أمينًا للخونة.\nوالوكالة من الأمانة، والمصطفى- عليه الصلاة والسلام- لم يقصد شيئًا من ذلك، ولا علم له بالواقعة، لولا أطلعَه تعالى، فلا نقص في اهتمامه، ولا درك (1) يلحقه. وبالجملة، فالآية خرجت مخرج التعريف بحقيقة الأمر في النازلة.\nثم نهاه عن الذبّ عنهم، فقال: وَلا تُجادِلْ عَنِ الَّذِينَ يَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَهُمْ\nوهم رهط ابن أبيرق السارق، قال السهيلي: هم بِشر وبشير ومُبشر وأُسَير، إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ خَوَّاناً\nأي: كثير الخيانة، أَثِيماً\nأي:\nمصرًا عليها، رُوِي أن طعمة هرب إلى مكة، وارتدَّ، ونَقَبَ حائطًا بها ليسرق أهله، فسقط الحائط عليه فقتله.\nويستفاد من الآية امتناع الجدال عمن عُلِمَت خيانتُه بالأحرى، أو كان مظنة الخيانة، كالكافر ونحوه. وكذا قال ابن العربي في أحكام القرآن في هذه الآية: إن النيابة عن المبطل المتهم في الخصومة لا تجوز، بدليل الآية. هـ.\nثم فَضحَ سرهم، فقال: يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ النَّاسِ\nأي: يستترون منهم، وَلا يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ اللَّهِ\nوهو أحق أن يستحيا منه ويُخاف وَهُوَ مَعَهُمْ\nلا يخفى عليه شيء، فلا طريق للنجاة إلا تَركُ ما يستُقبح، ويؤاخذ عليه سرًا وجهرًا. إِذْ يُبَيِّتُونَ\nأي: يدبرون ويُزَوِّرُون ما لا يَرْضى مِنَ الْقَوْلِ\nمن رمي البريء، والحلف الكاذب، وشهادة الزور، وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطاً\nلا يفوته شيء، ها أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ جادَلْتُمْ عَنْهُمْ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا\nودفعتم عنهم المعرة، فَمَنْ يُجادِلُ اللَّهَ عَنْهُمْ\nأي: مَن يُدافُع عنهم عذابه يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ، أَمْ مَنْ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكِيلًا\nيحميهم من عقاب الله، حين تُفضَح السرائر، ولا تنفع الأصحاب ولا العشائر.\nالإشارة: في الآية عتاب للقضاة والولاة إذا ظهرت صورة الحق بأمارات وقرائن، ثم تجمدوا على ظاهر الشريعة، حمية أو رشوة، فإن القضاء جُلّه فِراسة، وفيها عتاب لشيوخ التربية، إذا ظهر لهم عيب في المريد ستروه عليه حيَاء أو شفقة، ولذلك قالوا: شيخ التربية لا تليق به الشفقة، غير أنه لا يُعيَّن، بل يذكر في الجملة، وصاحب العيب يفهم نفسه، وفيها عتاب للفقراء إذا راقبوا الناس، وأظهروا لهم ما يُحبون، وأخفوا عنهم ما لا يرضون، لقوله-\nسبحانه-: يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ النَّاسِ ...\nالآية، بل ينبغي أن يكونوا بالعكس من هذا، قال بعضهم: إن الذين تكرهون مني، هو الذي يشتهيه قلبي. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم حضّهم على التوبة، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنَّا أَنْزَلْنا إِلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ بِالْحَقِّ لِتَحْكُمَ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ بِما أَراكَ اللَّهُ وَلا تَكُنْ لِلْخائِنِينَ خَصِيماً (105) وَاسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ إِنَّ اللَّهَ كانَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (106) وَلا تُجادِلْ عَنِ الَّذِينَ يَخْتانُونَ أَنْفُسَهُمْ إِنَّ اللَّهَ لا يُحِبُّ مَنْ كانَ خَوَّاناً أَثِيماً (107) يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ النَّاسِ وَلا يَسْتَخْفُونَ مِنَ اللَّهِ وَهُوَ مَعَهُمْ إِذْ يُبَيِّتُونَ ما لا يَرْضى مِنَ الْقَوْلِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِما يَعْمَلُونَ مُحِيطاً (108) ها أَنْتُمْ هؤُلاءِ جادَلْتُمْ عَنْهُمْ فِي الْحَياةِ الدُّنْيا فَمَنْ يُجادِلُ اللَّهَ عَنْهُمْ يَوْمَ الْقِيامَةِ أَمْ مَنْ يَكُونُ عَلَيْهِمْ وَكِيلاً (109)","quran_en":"We have sent down the Book to you with truth so that you may judge between people by what God has shown you. Do not plead for those who betray trust. Seek God's forgiveness: God is Forgiving, Merciful. Do not argue for people who betray their own souls; God does not love one persistently treacherous and sinful. They conceal themselves from people but do not conceal themselves from God, who is with them as they devise by night words He does not approve. God encompasses all that they do. Here you are, pleading their case in this life; but who will plead with God on their behalf on the Day of Resurrection, and who will act as their defender?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>arā<\/em> in “what God has shown you” means to acquaint someone with or make something known (<em>ʿirfāniyya<\/em>), not the construction denoting learned knowledge that takes three objects. Hence it does not govern three objects here.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reported occasion.<\/strong> God addresses the Prophet when he was about to defend Ṭuʿma ibn Ubayriq. The report says Ṭuʿma stole a coat of mail belonging to his neighbour Qatāda ibn al-Nuʿmān, which was in a sack of flour. Flour trickled through a tear and marked the route. He concealed the armour in a Jewish man's house. When searched, Ṭuʿma swore he neither possessed nor knew of it. The flour trail led to the Jewish man's home. The latter said Ṭuʿma had entrusted him with the armour, and other Jews testified to it. Ṭuʿma's clan, Banū Ẓafar, asked the Prophet to defend their man, asserting that otherwise he would be disgraced and the Jewish man vindicated. Relying on appearances and not knowing the actual events, the Prophet considered speaking on his behalf. Then the revelation came.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: arā in “what God has shown you” means to acquaint someone with or make something known ( ʿirfāniyya ), not the construction denoting learned knowledge that takes three objects. Hence it does not govern three objects here. Reported occasion. God addresses the Prophet when he was about to defend Ṭuʿma ibn Ubayriq. The report says Ṭuʿma stole a coat of mail belonging to his neighbour Qatāda ibn al-Nuʿmān, which was in a sack of flour. Flour trickled through a tear and marked the route. He concealed the armour in a Jewish man's house. When searched, Ṭuʿma swore he neither possessed nor knew of it. The flour trail led to the Jewish man's home. The latter said Ṭuʿma had entrusted him with the armour, and other Jews testified to it. Ṭuʿma's clan, Banū Ẓafar, asked the Prophet to defend their man, asserting that otherwise he would be disgraced and the Jewish man vindicated. Relying on appearances and not knowing the actual events, the Prophet considered speaking on his behalf. Then the revelation came."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>The Book was sent enveloped in truth so the Prophet could judge justly through what God makes known to him, whether by revelation or juridical effort (<em>ijtihād<\/em>). Ibn ʿAjība sees support here for analogy (<em>qiyās<\/em>) as recognised by the majority, although the exercise of independent judgment by prophets is itself disputed. “Do not be a disputant for traitors” means do not contend against the innocent for their sake or undertake their defence. “Ask forgiveness” concerns what had been contemplated, not a knowingly unjust judgment. He takes the verse as evidence that representing a protected non-Muslim is prohibited, as Ibn Shaʿbān also held, and records Ibn ʿĀt’s suggestion that Ibn Shaʿbān may have intended abstention as a recommendation, and Mālik ibn Dīnār's saying that being the trusted agent of traitors is itself sufficient treachery. Advocacy is a trust. Nevertheless the Chosen Prophet did not knowingly intend treachery or comprehend the hidden facts until God revealed them; his initial wish to help, resting on the apparent evidence, does not detract from his station or entail liability. The verse discloses the actual circumstances of the dispute.<\/p>\n<p>The people who “betray themselves” are Ṭuʿma's associates, identified by al-Suhaylī as Bishr, Bashīr, Mubashshir and Usayr. God does not love one repeatedly deceitful and persistent in sin. Ibn ʿAjība transmits that Ṭuʿma later fled to Mecca, apostasised and tried to burgle another house through its wall, which collapsed upon and killed him. From the verse Ibn ʿAjība infers, all the more, a prohibition on arguing for someone whose treachery is known, or who is an object of suspicion of treachery, such as an unbeliever and the like. He cites Ibn al-ʿArabī’s <em>Aḥkām al-Qurʾān<\/em>: “Representing a party in the wrong who is suspected in the dispute is not permissible, as evidenced by this verse.”<\/p>\n<p>People ashamed before their neighbours yet unashamed before God behave irrationally, since God is ever with them and knows what is hidden. Escape from His judgment comes only by abandoning what deserves censure inwardly and outwardly. To “devise words by night” means plotting false accusation of an innocent person, perjury and fabricated testimony. God encompasses their acts. They may have defended their associates in this world and concealed their disgrace, but no defender can protect them from judgment when concealed intentions are exposed and neither kinship nor friendship can avail them.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The Book was sent enveloped in truth so the Prophet could judge justly through what God makes known to him, whether by revelation or juridical effort ( ijtihād ). Ibn ʿAjība sees support here for analogy ( qiyās ) as recognised by the majority, although the exercise of independent judgment by prophets is itself disputed. “Do not be a disputant for traitors” means do not contend against the innocent for their sake or undertake their defence. “Ask forgiveness” concerns what had been contemplated, not a knowingly unjust judgment. He takes the verse as evidence that representing a protected non-Muslim is prohibited, as Ibn Shaʿbān also held, and records Ibn ʿĀt’s suggestion that Ibn Shaʿbān may have intended abstention as a recommendation, and Mālik ibn Dīnār's saying that being the trusted agent of traitors is itself sufficient treachery. Advocacy is a trust. Nevertheless the Chosen Prophet did not knowingly intend treachery or comprehend the hidden facts until God revealed them; his initial wish to help, resting on the apparent evidence, does not detract from his station or entail liability. The verse discloses the actual circumstances of the dispute. The people who “betray themselves” are Ṭuʿma's associates, identified by al-Suhaylī as Bishr, Bashīr, Mubashshir and Usayr. God does not love one repeatedly deceitful and persistent in sin. Ibn ʿAjība transmits that Ṭuʿma later fled to Mecca, apostasised and tried to burgle another house through its wall, which collapsed upon and killed him. From the verse Ibn ʿAjība infers, all the more, a prohibition on arguing for someone whose treachery is known, or who is an object of suspicion of treachery, such as an unbeliever and the like. He cites Ibn al-ʿArabī’s Aḥkām al-Qurʾān : “Representing a party in the wrong who is suspected in the dispute is not permissible, as evidenced by this verse.” People ashamed before their neighbours yet unashamed before God behave irrationally, since God is ever with them and knows what is hidden. Escape from His judgment comes only by abandoning what deserves censure inwardly and outwardly. To “devise words by night” means plotting false accusation of an innocent person, perjury and fabricated testimony. God encompasses their acts. They may have defended their associates in this world and concealed their disgrace, but no defender can protect them from judgment when concealed intentions are exposed and neither kinship nor friendship can avail them."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>The verses reproach judges and governors who discern signs and evidence pointing toward justice but rigidly cling to superficial legal appearance out of partisanship or bribery: judgment, says Ibn ʿAjība, relies considerably upon penetrating discernment (<em>firāsa<\/em>). They also admonish masters of formation who detect a defect in a disciple but conceal it out of embarrassment or pity. Hence their saying, “Pity does not befit a master of formation (<em>shaykh al-tarbiya<\/em>).” Yet the failing should be described generally without publicly naming the disciple, who recognises the admonition. Finally the verses censure Sufi poor (<em>fuqarāʾ<\/em>) who monitor people's approval, displaying what others like while hiding what would displease them. Instead, they should do the reverse of this. One of the people of the path said, “What you dislike in me is precisely what my heart desires.” God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"The verses reproach judges and governors who discern signs and evidence pointing toward justice but rigidly cling to superficial legal appearance out of partisanship or bribery: judgment, says Ibn ʿAjība, relies considerably upon penetrating discernment ( firāsa ). They also admonish masters of formation who detect a defect in a disciple but conceal it out of embarrassment or pity. Hence their saying, “Pity does not befit a master of formation ( shaykh al-tarbiya ).” Yet the failing should be described generally without publicly naming the disciple, who recognises the admonition. Finally the verses censure Sufi poor ( fuqarāʾ ) who monitor people's approval, displaying what others like while hiding what would displease them. Instead, they should do the reverse of this. One of the people of the path said, “What you dislike in me is precisely what my heart desires.” God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C557.1","page":557,"ar":"الدرك: التبعة.","en":"Darak: liability.","anchor":"ولا درك"}],"english_characters":4371,"arabic_characters":3879},{"id":"q4_cont_110","q":"4:110","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:110 · Repentance and mercy","source":"Cairo I:558","cairo":"I:558","source_page_first":558,"source_page_last":558,"ar":"وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُ ثُمَّ يَسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ يَجِدِ اللَّهَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (110)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً\nأي: ذنبًا قبيحًا يسوءُ به غيره، أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُ\nبذنب يختص به، أو من يعمل سوءًا بذنبٍ غيرِ الشرك، أو يظلم نفسه بالشرك، أو من يعمل سوءًا بالكبيرة، أو يظلم نفسه بالصغيرة، ثُمَّ يَسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ\nبالتوبة يَجِدِ اللَّهَ غَفُوراً\nلذنوبه رَحِيماً\nبقبول توبته، وفيه حث لطُعمَة وقومِه على التوبة والاستغفار.\nالإشارة: ومن يعمل سوءًا بالميل إلى الهوى، أو يظلم نفسه بالالتفات إلى السوى، أو من يعمل سوءًا بالهفوات والخطرات، أو يظلم نفسه بالغفلات والفترات، أو من يعمل سوءًا بالوقوف مع الكرامات وحلاوة الطاعات، أو يظلم نفسه بالقناعة من الترقي في الدرجات والمقامات، ثم يستغفر الله من حينه يجد الله غفورًا رحيمًا، حيث لم يُخرِجهُ من حضرته، ولم يتركه مع غفلته.\nثم عاتب رهط السارق على رميهم الغير بالسرقة، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً أَوْ يَظْلِمْ نَفْسَهُ ثُمَّ يَسْتَغْفِرِ اللَّهَ يَجِدِ اللَّهَ غَفُوراً رَحِيماً (110)","quran_en":"Whoever commits an evil or wrongs his own soul, then asks God to forgive him, will find God Forgiving, Merciful.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Evil may refer to a shameful sin that harms someone else, while wronging oneself denotes a private transgression. Alternatively evil can mean a sin other than associationism and wronging the soul can mean associationism; or evil denotes a major sin and self-wrong a lesser sin. Whoever then seeks forgiveness through repentance finds God forgiving sins and merciful in accepting repentance. The wording urges Ṭuʿma and his associates to repent.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Evil may refer to a shameful sin that harms someone else, while wronging oneself denotes a private transgression. Alternatively evil can mean a sin other than associationism and wronging the soul can mean associationism; or evil denotes a major sin and self-wrong a lesser sin. Whoever then seeks forgiveness through repentance finds God forgiving sins and merciful in accepting repentance. The wording urges Ṭuʿma and his associates to repent."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Whoever commits wrong by inclining to appetite, or wrongs the soul by turning to what is other than God (<em>siwā<\/em>); whoever stumbles in fleeting thoughts or falls into prolonged inattentiveness; whoever stops at extraordinary spiritual graces (<em>karāmāt<\/em>) or the sweetness of devotions, or wrongs himself by contentment with his present rank and refusal to advance through the stations—then immediately seeks pardon, finds God forgiving and merciful. He has not cast that person from His Presence or abandoned him to heedlessness.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever commits wrong by inclining to appetite, or wrongs the soul by turning to what is other than God ( siwā ); whoever stumbles in fleeting thoughts or falls into prolonged inattentiveness; whoever stops at extraordinary spiritual graces ( karāmāt ) or the sweetness of devotions, or wrongs himself by contentment with his present rank and refusal to advance through the stations—then immediately seeks pardon, finds God forgiving and merciful. He has not cast that person from His Presence or abandoned him to heedlessness."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":971,"arabic_characters":858},{"id":"q4_cont_111","q":"4:111–112","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:111–112 · The burden of wrongdoing","source":"Cairo I:558","cairo":"I:558–559","source_page_first":558,"source_page_last":559,"ar":"وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ إِثْماً فَإِنَّما يَكْسِبُهُ عَلى نَفْسِهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (111) وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ خَطِيئَةً أَوْ إِثْماً ثُمَّ يَرْمِ بِهِ بَرِيئاً فَقَدِ احْتَمَلَ بُهْتاناً وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً (112)\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ إِثْماً\nكسرقة أو يمين فاجرة، أو رمى غيره بجريمة، فَإِنَّما يَكْسِبُهُ عَلى نَفْسِهِ\nلا يتعدى ضررها إلى غيره، وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً\nبسرائر عباده حَكِيماً\nفي إمهالهم وسترهم، وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ خَطِيئَةً\nأي: جريمة تتعدى إلى ضرر غيره، أَوْ إِثْماً\nيختص بنفسه، ثُمَّ يَرْمِ بِهِ بَرِيئاً\nمنه، كما رمى طُعمَةُ زيدًا اليهوديِّ، فَقَدِ احْتَمَلَ بُهْتاناً\nوهو أن يبهت الرجل بما لم يفعل، وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً\nأي: ذنبًا ظاهرًا، لا يخفى قبحه وبشاعته.\nالإشارة: الإثم: ما حاك في الصدر وتلجلج فيه، ولم ينشرح إليه الصدر، وضده البر وهو ما ينشرح إليه الصدر ويطمئن إليه القلب، فكل من فعل شيئًا قد تلجلج قلبه منه ولم يقبله نقص من نوره، وأظلم قلبه منه، وإليه\nالإشارة بقوله: (ومن يكسب إثمًا..) الآية، أي: فإنما يُسَوِّدُ به نور نفسه وروحه، ومن تلبَّس بذنب أو عيب، ثم يرم به غيرَه من باب سُوءِ الظن (فقد احتمل بهتانًا وإثمًا مبينًا) لأن الواجب على المريد السائر أن يشهد الصفاء من غيره، ويُقصر النقصَ على نفسه، والواصل يرى الكمال في كل شيء لمعرفته في كل شيء. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم شهد لرسوله بالهداية والعناية، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ إِثْماً فَإِنَّما يَكْسِبُهُ عَلى نَفْسِهِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ عَلِيماً حَكِيماً (111) وَمَنْ يَكْسِبْ خَطِيئَةً أَوْ إِثْماً ثُمَّ يَرْمِ بِهِ بَرِيئاً فَقَدِ احْتَمَلَ بُهْتاناً وَإِثْماً مُبِيناً (112)","quran_en":"Whoever earns a sin earns it against himself; God is Knowing, Wise. Whoever commits a fault or sin and then casts its blame upon an innocent person has borne a slanderous falsehood and a manifest sin.","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>Theft, false oaths and laying a crime upon another are sins whose liability returns to the perpetrator. God knows the inward secrets of His servants and is wise in granting respite and concealing their faults. A “fault” (<em>khaṭīʾa<\/em>) may be an offence injuring another, whereas “sin” (<em>ithm<\/em>) can name wrongdoing confined to its perpetrator. If the guilty person then accuses an innocent person, as Ṭuʿma accused the Jewish man Zayd, he carries a <em>buhtān<\/em>: a slander which stuns the accused with a deed he never committed. He thereby bears an obviously abominable sin.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"Theft, false oaths and laying a crime upon another are sins whose liability returns to the perpetrator. God knows the inward secrets of His servants and is wise in granting respite and concealing their faults. A “fault” ( khaṭīʾa ) may be an offence injuring another, whereas “sin” ( ithm ) can name wrongdoing confined to its perpetrator. If the guilty person then accuses an innocent person, as Ṭuʿma accused the Jewish man Zayd, he carries a buhtān : a slander which stuns the accused with a deed he never committed. He thereby bears an obviously abominable sin."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Sin is what troubles the breast, remains unsettled there and meets no inward acceptance; its opposite, <em>birr<\/em> (righteous goodness), expands the breast and brings the heart peace. One who undertakes an action rejected by the inward conscience darkens the light of the soul and spirit. If he then projects his own defect upon another through suspicion, he compounds wrongdoing with false accusation. The traveller on the path should see purity in other people and locate the shortcoming in himself. The one who has arrived (<em>wāṣil<\/em>) sees perfection everywhere because the One truly recognised is seen through everything. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Sin is what troubles the breast, remains unsettled there and meets no inward acceptance; its opposite, birr (righteous goodness), expands the breast and brings the heart peace. One who undertakes an action rejected by the inward conscience darkens the light of the soul and spirit. If he then projects his own defect upon another through suspicion, he compounds wrongdoing with false accusation. The traveller on the path should see purity in other people and locate the shortcoming in himself. The one who has arrived ( wāṣil ) sees perfection everywhere because the One truly recognised is seen through everything. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1197,"arabic_characters":1242},{"id":"q4_cont_113","q":"4:113","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:113 · Divine protection and instruction","source":"Cairo I:559","cairo":"I:559–560","source_page_first":559,"source_page_last":560,"ar":"وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لَهَمَّتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ أَنْ يُضِلُّوكَ وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلاَّ أَنْفُسَهُمْ وَما يَضُرُّونَكَ مِنْ شَيْءٍ وَأَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَعَلَّمَكَ ما لَمْ تَكُنْ تَعْلَمُ وَكانَ فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ عَظِيماً (113)\nقلت: الجارّ في قوله: (من شيء) ، في موضع نصب على المصدر، أي: لا يضرونك شيئًا من الضرر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ\nبالعصمة ورحمته بالعناية، لَهَمَّتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ\nوهم رهط السارق أَنْ يُضِلُّوكَ\nعن القضاء بالحق، مع علمهم بالقصة، لكن سبقت العناية، وحفت الرعاية، فلم تخرج من عين الهداية. وليس المراد نفي همهم لأنه وقع، إنما المراد نفي تأثيره فيه، وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلَّا أَنْفُسَهُمْ\nلعوده عليهم، وَما يَضُرُّونَكَ مِنْ شَيْءٍ\nلأن الله عصمك، وما خطر ببالك من المجادلة عنهم، كان اعتمادًا منك على ظاهر الأمر، وإنما أُمرتَ أن تحكم بالظواهر، والله يتولى السرائر.\nوَأَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ\nأي: القرآن، وَالْحِكْمَةَ\nما نطقتَ به من الحِكَم، وَعَلَّمَكَ ما لَمْ تَكُنْ تَعْلَمُ\nمن خفيات الأمور، التي لم تطلع عليها، أو من أمور الدين والأحكام، وَكانَ فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ عَظِيماً\nولا فضل أعظم من النبوة، لا سيما وقد فضَّله على كافة الخلق وأرسله إلى كافة الناس، وهدى الله على يديه ما لم يَهدِ على يدِ أحد من الأنبياء قبله، إلى غير ذلك من الفضائل التي تفوت الحصر.\nالإشارة: لولا أن الله تفضل على أوليائه بسابق العناية، وحفَّت بهم منه الكلاءة والرعاية، لأضلتهم العموم عن عين التحقيق، ولأتلفتهم القواطع عن سلوك الطريق، لكن مَن سبقت له العناية لا يصيبه سهمُ الجناية، فثبَّتَ أقدامهم على سير الطريق، حتى أظهر لهم معالم التحقيق، فكشف عن قلوبهم رين الحجاب، حتى فهموا أسرار الكتاب، ونبع من قلوبهم ينابيع الحِكَم والأسرار، واطلعوا على علوم لم يُحِط بها كتاب ولا دفتر، فحازوا في الدارين خيرًا جسيمًا، وكان فضل الله عليهم عظيمًا.\nولما ظهرت السرقة على طعمة، كثر فى شأنه التناجي والخوض فيما لا يعني، فنهاهم الحقّ عن ذلك فقال:","quran_ar":"وَلَوْلا فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ وَرَحْمَتُهُ لَهَمَّتْ طائِفَةٌ مِنْهُمْ أَنْ يُضِلُّوكَ وَما يُضِلُّونَ إِلاَّ أَنْفُسَهُمْ وَما يَضُرُّونَكَ مِنْ شَيْءٍ وَأَنْزَلَ اللَّهُ عَلَيْكَ الْكِتابَ وَالْحِكْمَةَ وَعَلَّمَكَ ما لَمْ تَكُنْ تَعْلَمُ وَكانَ فَضْلُ اللَّهِ عَلَيْكَ عَظِيماً (113)","quran_en":"Were it not for God's grace and mercy upon you, a party of them would have tried to lead you astray. Yet they mislead none but themselves and cannot harm you at all. God sent down to you the Book and wisdom and taught you what you had not known; God's favour toward you is immense.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: the expression “in anything” (<em>min shayʾ<\/em>) has the syntactic force of an accusative verbal noun, “not the least amount of harm.” God's grace upon the Prophet consists in safeguarding from error (<em>ʿiṣma<\/em>) and His mercy in providential care. The thief's associates, although aware of the events, tried to divert judgment from the truth. God's prior care prevented their plan from affecting the Prophet. The verse does not deny that they intended deception; it denies that the intention achieved its effect. Their error returns upon themselves. The contemplated defence rested only on the apparent circumstances, which is what the Prophet is charged to judge; God knows the inner realities. The Book is the Qurʾān, wisdom includes the wisdom voiced by the Prophet, and what was taught includes hidden matters and ordinances not previously known. Among the immeasurable divine favours upon him, none is greater than prophethood, above all when accompanied by his pre-eminence among creation, universal mission and the guidance God brought about through him as He had not through any prophet before him.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the expression “in anything” ( min shayʾ ) has the syntactic force of an accusative verbal noun, “not the least amount of harm.” God's grace upon the Prophet consists in safeguarding from error ( ʿiṣma ) and His mercy in providential care. The thief's associates, although aware of the events, tried to divert judgment from the truth. God's prior care prevented their plan from affecting the Prophet. The verse does not deny that they intended deception; it denies that the intention achieved its effect. Their error returns upon themselves. The contemplated defence rested only on the apparent circumstances, which is what the Prophet is charged to judge; God knows the inner realities. The Book is the Qurʾān, wisdom includes the wisdom voiced by the Prophet, and what was taught includes hidden matters and ordinances not previously known. Among the immeasurable divine favours upon him, none is greater than prophethood, above all when accompanied by his pre-eminence among creation, universal mission and the guidance God brought about through him as He had not through any prophet before him."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Without the prior divine grace and protection surrounding God's saints, ordinary society would turn them from direct verification and attachments would destroy their progress along the path. When divine care precedes a person, the arrow of wrongdoing does not strike him. God makes their feet firm until landmarks of realised knowledge appear, removes the tarnish of veils from their hearts, opens to them the secrets of the Book, and causes fountains of wisdom and mystery to spring from them. They become acquainted with sciences not encompassed by book or register. Thus they acquire great good in both worlds; God's favour toward them is immense. After Ṭuʿma's theft became known, the text turns to the excessive whispering about his affair.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Without the prior divine grace and protection surrounding God's saints, ordinary society would turn them from direct verification and attachments would destroy their progress along the path. When divine care precedes a person, the arrow of wrongdoing does not strike him. God makes their feet firm until landmarks of realised knowledge appear, removes the tarnish of veils from their hearts, opens to them the secrets of the Book, and causes fountains of wisdom and mystery to spring from them. They become acquainted with sciences not encompassed by book or register. Thus they acquire great good in both worlds; God's favour toward them is immense. After Ṭuʿma's theft became known, the text turns to the excessive whispering about his affair."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":1850,"arabic_characters":1833},{"id":"q4_cont_114","q":"4:114","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:114 · Private talk, charity and reconciliation","source":"Cairo I:560","cairo":"I:560–561","source_page_first":560,"source_page_last":561,"ar":"لا خَيْرَ فِي كَثِيرٍ مِنْ نَجْواهُمْ إِلاَّ مَنْ أَمَرَ بِصَدَقَةٍ أَوْ مَعْرُوفٍ أَوْ إِصْلاحٍ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ فَسَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِ أَجْراً عَظِيماً (114)\nقلت: إن كان المراد بالنجوى الكلام الخفي فالاستثناء منقطع، وقد يكون متصلاً على حذف مضاف أي: إلا نجوى مَن أمر ... الخ. وإن كان المراد بالنجوى الجماعة المتناجين، فالاستثناء متصل. قاله ابن جزي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله محرّضًا على الصمت: لا خَيْرَ فِي كَثِيرٍ مما يتناجون به في شأن السارق أو غيره، بل لا خير في الكلام بأسره إِلَّا مَنْ أَمَرَ بِصَدَقَةٍ واجبة أو تطوعية، فله مثل أجره، أَوْ مَعْرُوفٍ وهو: ما يستحسنه الشرع، ويوافقه العقل، كالقرض، وإغاثة الملهوف، وتعليم الجاهل، وإرشاد الضال، وغير ذلك من أنواع المعروف. أو أمر بإصلاح بَيْنَ النَّاسِ، أي: إصلاح ذات البين، كإصلاح بين طعمة واليهودي وغيرهما.\nقال مجاهد: (هي عامة للناس) ، يريد أنه لا خير فيما يتناجى فيه الناس، ويخوضون فيه من الحديث، إلا ما كان من أعمال الخير.\nوَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ أي: الصدقة، والمعروف والإصلاح، ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ أي: مُخلصًا لله فَسَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِ أَجْراً عَظِيماً وخيرًا جسيمًا. قال البيضاوي: بَنَى الكلامَ على الأمر، ورتَّب الجزاء على الفعل، ليدل على أنه لما دخل الآمر في زمرة الخَيّرين كان الفاعل أدخل فيهم، وأن العمدة والغرض هو الفعل، واعتبار الأمر من حيث إنه وصلة إليه. وقيد الفعل بأن يكون لطلب مرضاة الله لأن الأعمال بالنيات، وإن من فعل خيرًا رياء وسمعة، لم يستحق بها من الله أجرًا، ووصف الأجر بالعظم تنبيهًا على حقارة مافات في جنبه من أغراض الدنيا. هـ.\nالإشارة: في الآية حثٌّ على الصمت، وهو ركن قوي في طريق التصوف، وهو أحد الأركان الأربعة التي هي: العزلة والجوع والسهر، فهذه طريق أهل البداية، ومن لا بداية له لا نهاية له، وقالوا: بقدر ما يصمت اللسان يعمر الجنان، وبقدر ما كان يتكلم اللسان يخرب الجنان. وقالوا أيضًا: إذا كثر العلمُ قلَّ الكلام، وإذا قل العلم كثر الكلام. وقالوا أيضًا: من عرف الله كَلَّ لسانهُ. وقيل لبعض العلماء: هل العلم فيما سلف أكثر، أو اليوم أكثر؟ قال:\nالعلم فيما سلف أكثر، والكلامُ اليومَ أكثر.\nوفي قوله: وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ.. إشارة إلى أن العمل أشرف من العلم بلا عمل. والله تعالى أعلم.\nثم نزل فى شأن طعمة، لما هرب وارتد مشركا:","quran_ar":"لا خَيْرَ فِي كَثِيرٍ مِنْ نَجْواهُمْ إِلاَّ مَنْ أَمَرَ بِصَدَقَةٍ أَوْ مَعْرُوفٍ أَوْ إِصْلاحٍ بَيْنَ النَّاسِ وَمَنْ يَفْعَلْ ذلِكَ ابْتِغاءَ مَرْضاتِ اللَّهِ فَسَوْفَ نُؤْتِيهِ أَجْراً عَظِيماً (114)","quran_en":"There is no good in much of their private whispering, except in the speech of one who enjoins charity, an acknowledged good, or reconciliation among people. Whoever does this seeking God's pleasure, We shall grant a tremendous reward.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>If <em>najwā<\/em> denotes whispered speech, the exception “except one who commands...” is grammatically disconnected unless a noun is understood: “except the confidential speech of someone who commands...” If it denotes the company of whisperers, the exception is connected. This is Ibn Juzayy's observation.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"If najwā denotes whispered speech, the exception “except one who commands...” is grammatically disconnected unless a noun is understood: “except the confidential speech of someone who commands...” If it denotes the company of whisperers, the exception is connected. This is Ibn Juzayy's observation."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>God encourages silence. There is no good in much of their private talk about the thief or others; indeed, Ibn ʿAjība says, there is no good in speech as a whole except when one enjoins charity, obligatory or voluntary, recognised right action (<em>maʿrūf<\/em>), or reconciliation. One who enjoins charity has a reward equal to that of its giver. <em>Maʿrūf<\/em> is what the revealed law approves and sound reason recognises, such as lending, rescuing someone in distress, educating the ignorant or guiding the lost. Reconciling those in conflict includes the dispute involving Ṭuʿma and the Jewish man. Mujāhid is cited for interpreting the verse universally, addressing conversations among all people. Whoever actually performs charity, beneficence or peacemaking, seeking God's satisfaction with sincerity, receives an abundant reward.<\/p>\n<p>Al-Bayḍāwī notes the rhetoric: the verse praises commanding good yet attaches the reward to <em>doing<\/em> it. The one who enjoins good is included among the good, but the performer merits that description even more; commanding is important because it leads to action. Seeking God's pleasure is made a condition because actions are judged by intentions. A deed performed for display or reputation does not earn this divine reward. Calling that reward tremendous draws attention to how paltry worldly objectives are beside it.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"God encourages silence. There is no good in much of their private talk about the thief or others; indeed, Ibn ʿAjība says, there is no good in speech as a whole except when one enjoins charity, obligatory or voluntary, recognised right action ( maʿrūf ), or reconciliation. One who enjoins charity has a reward equal to that of its giver. Maʿrūf is what the revealed law approves and sound reason recognises, such as lending, rescuing someone in distress, educating the ignorant or guiding the lost. Reconciling those in conflict includes the dispute involving Ṭuʿma and the Jewish man. Mujāhid is cited for interpreting the verse universally, addressing conversations among all people. Whoever actually performs charity, beneficence or peacemaking, seeking God's satisfaction with sincerity, receives an abundant reward. Al-Bayḍāwī notes the rhetoric: the verse praises commanding good yet attaches the reward to doing it. The one who enjoins good is included among the good, but the performer merits that description even more; commanding is important because it leads to action. Seeking God's pleasure is made a condition because actions are judged by intentions. A deed performed for display or reputation does not earn this divine reward. Calling that reward tremendous draws attention to how paltry worldly objectives are beside it."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Silence is a strong pillar of Sufi formation and one of its four disciplines, joined by solitude, hunger and vigil. These mark the way of beginners; one without a beginning will have no completion. They say that the garden of the heart is cultivated as the tongue becomes quiet and is devastated as speech proliferates. As knowledge increases, talk decreases; with less knowledge, words abound. Whoever knows God finds the tongue exhausted before Him. Asked whether there had been more knowledge in former times or now, a scholar replied, “Knowledge was more abundant before, but speech is more abundant today.” The phrase “whoever does this” also indicates the superiority of action over learning that never produces practice. God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Silence is a strong pillar of Sufi formation and one of its four disciplines, joined by solitude, hunger and vigil. These mark the way of beginners; one without a beginning will have no completion. They say that the garden of the heart is cultivated as the tongue becomes quiet and is devastated as speech proliferates. As knowledge increases, talk decreases; with less knowledge, words abound. Whoever knows God finds the tongue exhausted before Him. Asked whether there had been more knowledge in former times or now, a scholar replied, “Knowledge was more abundant before, but speech is more abundant today.” The phrase “whoever does this” also indicates the superiority of action over learning that never produces practice. God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2380,"arabic_characters":2041},{"id":"q4_cont_115","q":"4:115–116","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:115–116 · Opposing the Messenger and the believers","source":"Cairo I:561","cairo":"I:561–562","source_page_first":561,"source_page_last":562,"ar":"وَمَنْ يُشاقِقِ الرَّسُولَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ الْهُدى وَيَتَّبِعْ غَيْرَ سَبِيلِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ نُوَلِّهِ ما تَوَلَّى وَنُصْلِهِ جَهَنَّمَ وَساءَتْ مَصِيراً (115) إِنَّ اللَّهَ لاَ يَغْفِرُ أَنْ يُشْرَكَ بِهِ وَيَغْفِرُ مَا دُونَ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَمَنْ يُشْرِكْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلالاً بَعِيداً (116)\nقلت: المشاقة: المخالفة والمباعدة، كأنَّ كل واحد من المتخالفين في شق غير شقّ الآخر.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ يخالف الرَّسُولَ ويتباعد عنه مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ الْهُدى أي:\nبعد ما تحقق أنه على الهدى بالوقوف على المعجزات، فيترك طريق الحق وَيَتَّبِعْ غَيْرَ سَبِيلِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ أي: يسلك غير ما هم عليه، من اعتقاد أو عمل. نُوَلِّهِ ما تَوَلَّى أي: نتركه مع ما تولى، ونجعله وليًّا له، ونُخَلِّي بينه وبين ما اختاره من الضلالة، وَنُصْلِهِ جَهَنَّمَ أي: ندخله فيها، ونشويه بها، وَساءَتْ مَصِيراً أي: قبحت مصيرا جهنم التي يصير إليها. والآية تَدُل على حرمة مخالفة الإجماع، لأن الله رتَّب الوعيد الشديد على مشاقة الرسول، واتباع غير سبيل المؤمنين، وكل منهما محرم، وإذا كان اتباع غير سبيلهم محرمًا كان اتباع سبيلهم واجبًا، انظر البيضاوي.\nثم نزل في طُعمة لما ارتد مشركًا: إِنَّ اللَّهَ لاَ يَغْفِرُ أَنْ يُشْرَكَ بِهِ وَيَغْفِرُ مَا دُونَ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وقيل: كرر للتأكيد تقبيحًا لشأن الشرك، وقيل: أتى شيخ إلى رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم فقال: إني شيخ منهمك في الذنوب إلا أني لم أشرك بالله شيئًا منذ عرفتُه وآمنتُ به، ولم أتخذ من دونه وليًا، ولم أوقع المعاصي جرأة، وما توهمت طرفة عين أني أُعجز الله هربًا، وإني لنادم تائب، فما ترى حالي عند الله؟ فنزلت. وَمَنْ يُشْرِكْ بِاللَّهِ، فَقَدْ ضَلَّ عن الحق ضَلالًا بَعِيداً لأن الشّرك أقبح أنواع الضلالة، وأبعدها عن الثواب والاستقامة، وإنما ذكر في الآية الأولى. فَقَدِ افْتَرى لأنها متصلة بقصة أهل الكتاب، ومنشأ شركهم نوع افتراء، وهو دعوى الشيء على الله.\nقاله البيضاوي.\nالإشارة: كل من خالف شيخه، وسلك طريقًا غير طريقه ولاه الله ما تولى، واستدرجه من حيث لا يشعر، وقد تؤخر العقوبة عنه فيقول: لو كان هذا فيه سوء أدب مع الله، لقطع الإمداد وأوجب البعاد، وقد يقطع عنه من حيث لا يشعر، ولو لم يكن إلا وتخليته وما يريد. وبالجملة: فالخروج عن مشايخ التربية والانتقال عنهم، ولو إلى من هو أكمل في زعمه، بعد ما ظهر له الفتح والهداية على يديه طردٌ وبعدٌ، وإفساد لبذرة الإرادة، فلا نتيجة له أصلاً. والله تعالى أعلم. وبالله التوفيق.\nثم قبّح شأن الشرك، وشنّع قبحه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يُشاقِقِ الرَّسُولَ مِنْ بَعْدِ ما تَبَيَّنَ لَهُ الْهُدى وَيَتَّبِعْ غَيْرَ سَبِيلِ الْمُؤْمِنِينَ نُوَلِّهِ ما تَوَلَّى وَنُصْلِهِ جَهَنَّمَ وَساءَتْ مَصِيراً (115) إِنَّ اللَّهَ لاَ يَغْفِرُ أَنْ يُشْرَكَ بِهِ وَيَغْفِرُ مَا دُونَ ذلِكَ لِمَنْ يَشاءُ وَمَنْ يُشْرِكْ بِاللَّهِ فَقَدْ ضَلَّ ضَلالاً بَعِيداً (116)","quran_en":"Whoever opposes the Messenger after guidance has become clear to him, and follows a path other than that of the believers, We shall leave him to what he has chosen and consign him to Hell—an evil destination. God does not forgive associating partners with Him, but forgives what is less than that to whomever He wills. Whoever associates anything with God has strayed very far indeed.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>mushāqqa<\/em> is opposition and estrangement, as if the two opponents occupied different sides (<em>shiqq<\/em>) of a division. Whoever departs from the Messenger after recognising his guidance through his miracles, and follows beliefs or conduct contrary to the believers' path, is left to the allegiance he selected and to the misguidance he embraced. Ibn ʿAjība, following al-Bayḍāwī, treats the two prohibited acts—opposing the Messenger and following a different communal path—as evidence that violating authoritative consensus (<em>ijmāʿ<\/em>) is forbidden, and consequently that the believers' agreed path is binding.<\/p>\n<p>The next verse is also said to have been revealed concerning Ṭuʿma after his apostasy, or to repeat the condemnation of associationism for emphasis. Another report concerns an old man who asked the Prophet about long-standing sin, explaining that he had never associated partners with God, had never relied on another patron, had not sinned in deliberate defiance, had always known he could not elude God, and was repentant. The verse was revealed, according to this report. Associationism constitutes the furthest departure from truth and rectitude. Al-Bayḍāwī explains that the earlier use of “fabricated a monstrous lie” fits the preceding controversy with the People of the Book, whose particular error involved making a claim about God.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: mushāqqa is opposition and estrangement, as if the two opponents occupied different sides ( shiqq ) of a division. Whoever departs from the Messenger after recognising his guidance through his miracles, and follows beliefs or conduct contrary to the believers' path, is left to the allegiance he selected and to the misguidance he embraced. Ibn ʿAjība, following al-Bayḍāwī, treats the two prohibited acts—opposing the Messenger and following a different communal path—as evidence that violating authoritative consensus ( ijmāʿ ) is forbidden, and consequently that the believers' agreed path is binding. The next verse is also said to have been revealed concerning Ṭuʿma after his apostasy, or to repeat the condemnation of associationism for emphasis. Another report concerns an old man who asked the Prophet about long-standing sin, explaining that he had never associated partners with God, had never relied on another patron, had not sinned in deliberate defiance, had always known he could not elude God, and was repentant. The verse was revealed, according to this report. Associationism constitutes the furthest departure from truth and rectitude. Al-Bayḍāwī explains that the earlier use of “fabricated a monstrous lie” fits the preceding controversy with the People of the Book, whose particular error involved making a claim about God."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Whoever opposes his shaykh and follows a path other than his, God leaves him to what he has chosen and gradually draws him toward ruin without his noticing. A person may suppose that continuing spiritual gifts prove no error of conduct occurred, even though the gifts have been cut off without his awareness—or even though he is simply being left alone to his desires. In Ibn ʿAjība’s account, leaving a master through whom openings and guidance have appeared, even for another imagined to be more perfect, is expulsion and estrangement, corrupts the seed of aspiration, and yields no result at all. [Translator’s note: this claim concerns Ibn ʿAjība’s school of discipleship and should be distinguished from the independent juristic limits of a teacher’s authority.] God knows best.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Whoever opposes his shaykh and follows a path other than his, God leaves him to what he has chosen and gradually draws him toward ruin without his noticing. A person may suppose that continuing spiritual gifts prove no error of conduct occurred, even though the gifts have been cut off without his awareness—or even though he is simply being left alone to his desires. In Ibn ʿAjība’s account, leaving a master through whom openings and guidance have appeared, even for another imagined to be more perfect, is expulsion and estrangement, corrupts the seed of aspiration, and yields no result at all. [Translator’s note: this claim concerns Ibn ʿAjība’s school of discipleship and should be distinguished from the independent juristic limits of a teacher’s authority.] God knows best."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":2136,"arabic_characters":2225},{"id":"q4_cont_117","q":"4:117–121","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:117–121 · False objects of devotion and Satan’s promises","source":"Cairo I:562","cairo":"I:562–563","source_page_first":562,"source_page_last":563,"ar":"إِنْ يَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِهِ إِلاَّ إِناثاً وَإِنْ يَدْعُونَ إِلاَّ شَيْطاناً مَرِيداً (117) لَعَنَهُ اللَّهُ وَقالَ لَأَتَّخِذَنَّ مِنْ عِبادِكَ نَصِيباً مَفْرُوضاً (118) وَلَأُضِلَّنَّهُمْ وَلَأُمَنِّيَنَّهُمْ وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُبَتِّكُنَّ آذانَ الْأَنْعامِ وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُغَيِّرُنَّ خَلْقَ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ يَتَّخِذِ الشَّيْطانَ وَلِيًّا مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ فَقَدْ خَسِرَ خُسْراناً مُبِيناً (119) يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَما يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطانُ إِلاَّ غُرُوراً (120) أُولئِكَ مَأْواهُمْ جَهَنَّمُ وَلا يَجِدُونَ عَنْها مَحِيصاً (121)\nقلت: المَرِيد والمارد هو الذي لا يعلق بخير، وأصل التركيب للملابسة، ومنه: صرح ممرَّد، وغلام أمرد، وشجرة مردى، أي: سقط ورقها. قاله البيضاوي. هـ. وقيل: المريد: الشديد العاتي، الخارج عن الطاعة.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: إِنْ يَدْعُونَ: ما يعبدون مِنْ دُونِهِ تعالى إِلَّا إِناثاً، كاللات والعزى ومناة، فإن ألفاظها مؤنثة عندهم، أو لأنها جوامد لا تعقل، فهي منفعلة لا فاعلة، ومن حق المعبود أن يكون فاعلاً غير منفعل، أو يريد الملائكة لأنهم كانوا يعبدونها، ويزعمون أنها بنات الله، وما يعبدون في الحقيقة إِلَّا شَيْطاناً مَرِيداً عاصيًا، لأنه هو الذي أمرهم بها، وأغراهم عليها، وكان يكلمهم من أجوافها.\nثم وصفه بأوصاف تُوجب التنفير عنه فقال: لَعَنَهُ اللَّهُ أي: أبعده من رحمته، وَقالَ لَأَتَّخِذَنَّ مِنْ عِبادِكَ نَصِيباً مَفْرُوضاً أي: مقطوعًا فرضته لنفسي، من قولهم: فرض له في العطاء، أي: قطع، وَلَأُضِلَّنَّهُمْ عن الحق وَلَأُمَنِّيَنَّهُمْ الأماني الباطلة، كطول الحياة، وألاَّ بعث ولا عقاب، وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُبَتِّكُنَّ آذانَ الْأَنْعامِ أي: يشقونها لتحريم ما أحل الله، وهي عبارة عما كانت العرب تفعل بالبحائر والسوائب، وإشارة إلى تحريم كل ما أحل الله، ونقص كل ما خلق الله كاملاً بالفعل أو بالقوة، وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُغَيِّرُنَّ خَلْقَ اللَّهِ، صورة، أو صفة، فيندرج فيه خصاء العبيد والوشم، والتنمص- وهو نتف الحاجب-.\nزاد البيضاوي: واللواط، والمساحقة، وعبادة الشمس والقمر، وتغيير فطرة الله التي هي الإسلام، واستعمال الجوارح والقوى فيما لا يعود على النفس كمالاً ولا يوجب لها من الله زلفى. وعموم اللفظ يقتضي منع الخِصاء مطلقًا، لكن الفقهاء رخصوا في خصاء البهائم للحاجة، والجُمل الأربع حكاية عما ذكره الشيطان نطقًا، أو أتاه فعلاً. هـ.\nثم حذّر منه فقال: وَمَنْ يَتَّخِذِ الشَّيْطانَ وَلِيًّا مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ باتباعه فيما أمره به دون ما أمر الله به، فَقَدْ خَسِرَ خُسْراناً مُبِيناً واضحًا حيث ضيع رأس ماله، وأبدل بمكانه من الجنة مكانه من النار. يَعِدُهُمْ أي: الشيطان، أمورًا لا تُنجز لهم، وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ أماني لا تعطى لهم، وَما يَعِدُهُمُ أي: الشَّيْطانُ إِلَّا غُرُوراً، وهو إظهار النفع فيما فيه الضرر، فكان يوسوس لهم أنهم على الحق وأنهم أولى بالجنة، إلى غير ذلك من أنواع الغرور، أُولئِكَ المغرورون مَأْواهُمْ جَهَنَّمُ أي: هي منزلهم ومقامهم، وَلا يَجِدُونَ عَنْها مَحِيصاً أي: مهربًا ولا معدلاً. من حاص يحيص: إذا عدل.\nالإشارة: ما أحببتَ شيئاً إلا كنت له عبدًا، فاحذر أن تكون ممن يعبد من دون الله إناثًا، إن كنت تحب نفسك، وتؤثر هواها على حق مولاها، أو تكون عبد المرأة أو الخميصة (1) أو البهيمة، أو غير ذلك من الشهوات التي أنت تحبها، واحذر أيضًا أن تكون من نصيب الشيطان بإيحاشك إلى الكريم المنان، وفي الحِكَم:\n«إذا علمت أن الشيطان لا يغفل عنك، فلا تغفل أنت عمن ناصيتك بيده» . فاشتغل بمحبة الحبيب، يكفيك عداوة العدو، فاتخذ الله وليًا وصاحبًا، ودع الشيطان جانبًا، غِب عن الشيطان باستغراقك في حضرة العِيان.\nوبالله التوفيق.\nثم ذكر ضد أهل الشرك، فقال:","quran_ar":"إِنْ يَدْعُونَ مِنْ دُونِهِ إِلاَّ إِناثاً وَإِنْ يَدْعُونَ إِلاَّ شَيْطاناً مَرِيداً (117) لَعَنَهُ اللَّهُ وَقالَ لَأَتَّخِذَنَّ مِنْ عِبادِكَ نَصِيباً مَفْرُوضاً (118) وَلَأُضِلَّنَّهُمْ وَلَأُمَنِّيَنَّهُمْ وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُبَتِّكُنَّ آذانَ الْأَنْعامِ وَلَآمُرَنَّهُمْ فَلَيُغَيِّرُنَّ خَلْقَ اللَّهِ وَمَنْ يَتَّخِذِ الشَّيْطانَ وَلِيًّا مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ فَقَدْ خَسِرَ خُسْراناً مُبِيناً (119) يَعِدُهُمْ وَيُمَنِّيهِمْ وَما يَعِدُهُمُ الشَّيْطانُ إِلاَّ غُرُوراً (120) أُولئِكَ مَأْواهُمْ جَهَنَّمُ وَلا يَجِدُونَ عَنْها مَحِيصاً (121)","quran_en":"What they invoke apart from Him are only female beings, and what they invoke is a rebellious Satan, cursed by God. He said, “I shall take an appointed share of Your servants. I shall surely mislead them, fill them with vain hopes, command them to slit the ears of cattle, and command them to change God's creation.” Whoever takes Satan as a patron instead of God has plainly lost. Satan promises and tempts them with hopes, but his promises are deception. Their refuge is Hell, from which they shall find no escape.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>marīd<\/em> or <em>mārid<\/em> can describe something to which no good adheres. The root’s basic sense is [smoothness], with the examples of a smooth palace, a beardless youth and a tree whose leaves have fallen; this is attributed to al-Bayḍāwī. [Translator’s textual note: Cairo I:562 prints <em>li-l-mulābasa<\/em>, “association” or “intermingling,” as does the checked electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī. Abū al-Suʿūd on 4:117 explicitly has <em>li-l-malāsa<\/em>, “smoothness,” beside the same palace and bare-tree examples. Those examples and the exact earlier parallel establish the intended lexical explanation. The bracketed English adopts it openly; it does not claim that Cairo or an independently checked manuscript of Ibn ʿAjība prints the corrected word. See source check XII-S062.] Another explanation is defiant and intensely rebellious, departing from obedience.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: marīd or mārid can describe something to which no good adheres. The root’s basic sense is [smoothness], with the examples of a smooth palace, a beardless youth and a tree whose leaves have fallen; this is attributed to al-Bayḍāwī. [Translator’s textual note: Cairo I:562 prints li-l-mulābasa , “association” or “intermingling,” as does the checked electronic text of al-Bayḍāwī. Abū al-Suʿūd on 4:117 explicitly has li-l-malāsa , “smoothness,” beside the same palace and bare-tree examples. Those examples and the exact earlier parallel establish the intended lexical explanation. The bracketed English adopts it openly; it does not claim that Cairo or an independently checked manuscript of Ibn ʿAjība prints the corrected word. See source check XII-S062.] Another explanation is defiant and intensely rebellious, departing from obedience."},{"type":"exposition","title":"","html":"<p>The female entities invoked apart from God may be idols named with feminine nouns—al-Lāt, al-ʿUzzā and Manāt—or lifeless objects capable only of being acted upon, whereas divinity demands an agent, not something passively acted upon. Or the verse may mean the angels, falsely regarded by their worshippers as divine daughters. In reality such worship answers Satan, who encouraged it and, in some reports, spoke from the interior of the idols. God cursed Satan, distancing him from mercy. Satan claimed an allotted share of servants, resolved to mislead them and promised false hopes of long life, denial of resurrection or immunity from judgment.<\/p>\n<p>His command to slit livestock ears refers to the custom of marking <em>baḥīra<\/em> and <em>sāʾiba<\/em> animals and prohibiting what God permitted. By extension it refers to wrongfully forbidding permitted things and damaging an integrity God created, whether actually realised or latent. “Changing God's creation” may concern bodily alteration or change of attributes. Ibn ʿAjība lists castration of slaves, tattooing and plucking eyebrows; al-Bayḍāwī adds sodomy (<em>liwāṭ<\/em>) and female same-sex sexual activity (<em>musāḥaqa<\/em>), worship of the sun and moon, replacing the primordial disposition (<em>fiṭra<\/em>) of Islam, and employing limbs and capacities in ways that bring the soul no perfection or divine nearness. Although the verse's generality seems to prohibit castration, jurists made an exception for castrating animals where a recognised need existed. The four clauses reporting Satan's resolutions may recount words actually spoken or deeds through which the intentions were expressed.<\/p>\n<p>To take Satan as a protecting companion by obeying his commands in preference to God's is a manifest loss: the person forfeits the capital of life and substitutes a place in Hell for the promised place in Paradise. Satan makes promises he cannot fulfil, arouses desires for things never to be given, and disguises harm as benefit, including the delusion that his followers are right and best entitled to Paradise. Such people will find no turning away (<em>maḥīṣ<\/em>) from the consequence; <em>ḥāṣa<\/em> means to turn aside.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"The female entities invoked apart from God may be idols named with feminine nouns—al-Lāt, al-ʿUzzā and Manāt—or lifeless objects capable only of being acted upon, whereas divinity demands an agent, not something passively acted upon. Or the verse may mean the angels, falsely regarded by their worshippers as divine daughters. In reality such worship answers Satan, who encouraged it and, in some reports, spoke from the interior of the idols. God cursed Satan, distancing him from mercy. Satan claimed an allotted share of servants, resolved to mislead them and promised false hopes of long life, denial of resurrection or immunity from judgment. His command to slit livestock ears refers to the custom of marking baḥīra and sāʾiba animals and prohibiting what God permitted. By extension it refers to wrongfully forbidding permitted things and damaging an integrity God created, whether actually realised or latent. “Changing God's creation” may concern bodily alteration or change of attributes. Ibn ʿAjība lists castration of slaves, tattooing and plucking eyebrows; al-Bayḍāwī adds sodomy ( liwāṭ ) and female same-sex sexual activity ( musāḥaqa ), worship of the sun and moon, replacing the primordial disposition ( fiṭra ) of Islam, and employing limbs and capacities in ways that bring the soul no perfection or divine nearness. Although the verse's generality seems to prohibit castration, jurists made an exception for castrating animals where a recognised need existed. The four clauses reporting Satan's resolutions may recount words actually spoken or deeds through which the intentions were expressed. To take Satan as a protecting companion by obeying his commands in preference to God's is a manifest loss: the person forfeits the capital of life and substitutes a place in Hell for the promised place in Paradise. Satan makes promises he cannot fulfil, arouses desires for things never to be given, and disguises harm as benefit, including the delusion that his followers are right and best entitled to Paradise. Such people will find no turning away ( maḥīṣ ) from the consequence; ḥāṣa means to turn aside."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Whatever you love, you become its slave. Beware of serving “female idols” in the sense of loving the lower soul and preferring its appetite to the Lord's claim. Likewise do not become the slave of a woman, a fine garment, an animal, or any other object of appetite that you love. Nor yield to Satan's efforts to make you estranged from the Most Generous. The <em>Ḥikam<\/em> warns: “When you know that Satan does not neglect you, do not neglect the One who holds your forelock.” Occupy yourself with love of the Beloved: it suffices against the enmity of the enemy. Take God as intimate patron and companion, put Satan aside and become absent from his suggestions through immersion in the Presence of direct witnessing. God gives success.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Whatever you love, you become its slave. Beware of serving “female idols” in the sense of loving the lower soul and preferring its appetite to the Lord's claim. Likewise do not become the slave of a woman, a fine garment, an animal, or any other object of appetite that you love. Nor yield to Satan's efforts to make you estranged from the Most Generous. The Ḥikam warns: “When you know that Satan does not neglect you, do not neglect the One who holds your forelock.” Occupy yourself with love of the Beloved: it suffices against the enmity of the enemy. Take God as intimate patron and companion, put Satan aside and become absent from his suggestions through immersion in the Presence of direct witnessing. God gives success."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C563.1","page":563,"ar":"الخميصة: ثوب خز، أو صوف.","en":"Khamiṣa: a garment of khazz cloth or wool.","anchor":"الخميصة"}],"english_characters":3700,"arabic_characters":3190},{"id":"q4_cont_122","q":"4:122","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:122 · The promise to believers","source":"Cairo I:563","cairo":"I:563–564","source_page_first":563,"source_page_last":564,"ar":"وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ سَنُدْخِلُهُمْ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها أَبَداً وَعْدَ اللَّهِ حَقًّا وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ قِيلاً (122)\nقلت: (وعد اللهِ) مصدر، مؤكد لنفسه، أي: وعدهم وعدًا، و (حقًا) مؤكد لغيره، أي: لمضمون الجملة قبله.\nانظر البيضاوي.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا بالله ووحدوه، وَعَمِلُوا الأعمال الصَّالِحاتِ التي كلفوا بها سَنُدْخِلُهُمْ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها أَبَداً وعدهم بذلك وعدًا حقًا، وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ قِيلًا أي: لا أحدَ أصدقُ من الله في قوله. والمقصود من الآية معارضة المواعيد الشيطانية الكاذبة لقرنائه، بوعد الله الصادق لأوليائه، ترغيبًا في تحصيل أسبابه. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: والذين جمعوا بين توحيد عظمة الربوبية والقيام بوظائف العبودية سندخلهم جنةَ المعارف، تجري من تحتها أنهار العلوم، خالدين فيها أبدًا، وعداً حقاً وقولاً صدقاً. ومن أصدق من الله قيلا؟\nوهذا الوعد لا ينال بالأمانى مع البطالة والتواني وإنما ينال بالأعمال الصالحة والمقاصد الخالصة، كما قال تعالى:","quran_ar":"وَالَّذِينَ آمَنُوا وَعَمِلُوا الصَّالِحاتِ سَنُدْخِلُهُمْ جَنَّاتٍ تَجْرِي مِنْ تَحْتِهَا الْأَنْهارُ خالِدِينَ فِيها أَبَداً وَعْدَ اللَّهِ حَقًّا وَمَنْ أَصْدَقُ مِنَ اللَّهِ قِيلاً (122)","quran_en":"Those who believe and perform righteous works—We shall admit them to gardens beneath which rivers flow, abiding there forever: God's truthful promise. Whose word is truer than God's?","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: <em>waʿda llāhi<\/em> is a confirming verbal noun reinforcing its own action—“He promised them a promise.” <em>Ḥaqqan<\/em> confirms the substance of the preceding statement, as al-Bayḍāwī explains. Those who profess God's unity and perform the works imposed upon them are admitted to eternal gardens. No speech is more truthful than God's. The verse counterposes God's dependable promise to His intimates against the false promises Satan offers his associates, and encourages attaining the means of that promise.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: waʿda llāhi is a confirming verbal noun reinforcing its own action—“He promised them a promise.” Ḥaqqan confirms the substance of the preceding statement, as al-Bayḍāwī explains. Those who profess God's unity and perform the works imposed upon them are admitted to eternal gardens. No speech is more truthful than God's. The verse counterposes God's dependable promise to His intimates against the false promises Satan offers his associates, and encourages attaining the means of that promise."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Those who unite recognition of the oneness of majestic Lordship (<em>rubūbiyya<\/em>) with the actual duties of servanthood (<em>ʿubūdiyya<\/em>) will enter the garden of direct knowledges (<em>jannat al-maʿārif<\/em>), beneath which flow rivers of sciences, abiding there in enduring felicity. The promise is true, but it is not obtained through fantasies joined to idleness: it is acquired through upright works and purified intentions, as the following verse makes plain.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Those who unite recognition of the oneness of majestic Lordship ( rubūbiyya ) with the actual duties of servanthood ( ʿubūdiyya ) will enter the garden of direct knowledges ( jannat al-maʿārif ), beneath which flow rivers of sciences, abiding there in enduring felicity. The promise is true, but it is not obtained through fantasies joined to idleness: it is acquired through upright works and purified intentions, as the following verse makes plain."}],"cairo_notes":[],"english_characters":950,"arabic_characters":1008},{"id":"q4_cont_123","q":"4:123","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:123 · Merit, religion and accountability","source":"Cairo I:564","cairo":"I:564–565","source_page_first":564,"source_page_last":565,"ar":"لَيْسَ بِأَمانِيِّكُمْ وَلا أَمانِيِّ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ مَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً يُجْزَ بِهِ وَلا يَجِدْ لَهُ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَلِيًّا وَلا نَصِيراً (123)\nقلت: اسم ليس ضمير الأمر، أي: ليس الأمر بأمانيكم.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: لَيْسَ هذا الوعد الذي ذكرت لأهل الإيمان يُنَال بِأَمانِيِّكُمْ أي: تمنيكم أيها المسلمون، ولا بأماني أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ، أي: لا يكون ما تتمنون ولا ما يتمنى أهل الكتاب، بل يحكم الله بين عباده ويجازيهم بأعمالهم. رُوِي أن المسلمين وأهل الكتاب تفاخروا، فقال أهل الكتاب: نبينا قبل نبيكم، وكتابنا قبل كتابكم، ونحن أولى بالله منكم، وقال المسلمون: نحن أولى منكم، نبينا خاتم النبيين، وكتابنا يقضي على الكتب المتقدمة، فنزلت. وقيل: الخطاب مع المشركين، وهو قولهم: لا جنة ولا نار، أو قولهم: إن كان الأمر كما يزعم هؤلاء لنكونن خيرًا منهم وأحسن حالاً.\nوأماني أهل الكتاب: قولهم لَنْ تَمَسَّنَا النَّارُ إِلَّا أَيَّاماً مَعْدُوداتٍ ولَنْ يَدْخُلَ الْجَنَّةَ إِلَّا مَنْ كانَ هُوداً أَوْ نَصارى ثم قرر ذلك فقال: مَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً يُجْزَ بِهِ عاجلاً أو آجلاً لِما رُوِي أنه لمّا نزلت قال أبو بكر:\nمن ينجو مع هذا يا رسول الله، إن كنا مجزيين بكل سوء عملناه؟ فقال له- عليه الصلاة والسلام-: «أما تحزن؟\nأما تمرض؟ أما يصيبك اللأواء (1)؟ قال: بلى يا رسول الله، قال: هو ذلك» . فكل من عمل سوءًا جوزي به، وَلا يَجِدْ لَهُ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَلِيًّا يليه ويدفع عنه، وَلا نَصِيراً ينصره ويمنعه من عذاب الله.\nالإشارة: لا تُنال المراتب بالأماني الكاذبة والدعاوي الفارغة، وإنما تنال بالهمم العالية، والمجاهدات القوية، إنما تنال المقامات العالية بالأعمال الصالحة، والأحوال الصافية، وأنشدوا:\nبقدر الكدّ تُكتَسَبُ المَعَالِي ... من أراد العز سهر الليالي\nتُرِيدُ العزَّ ثُم تَنَامُ ليلاً ... يَغُوصُ البحر مَن طَلَبَ اللآلي\nولمّا نزل قوله تعالى: لَيْسَ بِأَمانِيِّكُمْ وَلا أَمانِيِّ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ ... الآية. قال أهل الكتاب: نحن وأنتم سواء، فأنزل الله تعالى:","quran_ar":"لَيْسَ بِأَمانِيِّكُمْ وَلا أَمانِيِّ أَهْلِ الْكِتابِ مَنْ يَعْمَلْ سُوءاً يُجْزَ بِهِ وَلا يَجِدْ لَهُ مِنْ دُونِ اللَّهِ وَلِيًّا وَلا نَصِيراً (123)","quran_en":"It is not according to your wishes, nor the wishes of the People of the Book. Whoever commits an evil shall receive its recompense, and will find no protecting friend or helper apart from God.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: the suppressed subject of <em>laysa<\/em> is “the matter,” so the sense is: “the matter is not governed by your wishes.” The promise given to believers is not attained by the Muslims' hopes alone or by those of the People of the Book. God will judge between servants by deeds. A report tells of mutual boasting: the People of the Book argued their prophets and scriptures preceded those of Muslims; Muslims claimed precedence because their Prophet was the Seal and their Book confirmed and governed earlier books. Another interpretation addresses polytheists who denied Paradise and Hell, or who imagined they would fare better than believers should the hereafter prove true. The People of the Book's wishful claims include that the Fire would touch them only briefly and that Paradise was reserved for Jews or Christians.<\/p>\n<p>Wrongdoing must be accounted for, whether now or later. Abū Bakr is reported to have feared, when the verse was revealed, that no one could be saved if every wrong brought retribution. The Prophet reminded him that sorrow, sickness and life's severe difficulties themselves answer part of that account. Outside God no guardian or helper can repel His judgment.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: the suppressed subject of laysa is “the matter,” so the sense is: “the matter is not governed by your wishes.” The promise given to believers is not attained by the Muslims' hopes alone or by those of the People of the Book. God will judge between servants by deeds. A report tells of mutual boasting: the People of the Book argued their prophets and scriptures preceded those of Muslims; Muslims claimed precedence because their Prophet was the Seal and their Book confirmed and governed earlier books. Another interpretation addresses polytheists who denied Paradise and Hell, or who imagined they would fare better than believers should the hereafter prove true. The People of the Book's wishful claims include that the Fire would touch them only briefly and that Paradise was reserved for Jews or Christians. Wrongdoing must be accounted for, whether now or later. Abū Bakr is reported to have feared, when the verse was revealed, that no one could be saved if every wrong brought retribution. The Prophet reminded him that sorrow, sickness and life's severe difficulties themselves answer part of that account. Outside God no guardian or helper can repel His judgment."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>High stations cannot be reached by empty wishing and boasts. They require lofty aspiration (<em>himam<\/em>), vigorous spiritual exertion (<em>mujāhada<\/em>), good works and purified states. Verses are recited:<\/p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Sublimity is won in proportion to the toil;<br>whoever seeks honour spends the nights awake.<br>You desire exaltation, yet sleep through the night—<br>the seeker of pearls must dive into the sea.<\/p>\n<\/blockquote>\n<p>According to the report, the People of the Book responded that Muslims and themselves had been made equal; the following verse then makes the distinctive requirements of belief and work clear.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"High stations cannot be reached by empty wishing and boasts. They require lofty aspiration ( himam ), vigorous spiritual exertion ( mujāhada ), good works and purified states. Verses are recited: Sublimity is won in proportion to the toil; whoever seeks honour spends the nights awake. You desire exaltation, yet sleep through the night— the seeker of pearls must dive into the sea. According to the report, the People of the Book responded that Muslims and themselves had been made equal; the following verse then makes the distinctive requirements of belief and work clear."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C564.1","page":564,"ar":"اللأواء: الشدة وضيق العيش.","en":"Laʾwāʾ: hardship and straitened living.","anchor":"اللأواء"}],"english_characters":1755,"arabic_characters":1748},{"id":"q4_cont_124","q":"4:124–126","s":4,"surah":"Al-Nisāʾ","title":"4:124–126 · Faithful works and the Garden","source":"Cairo I:565–566","cairo":"I:565–566","source_page_first":565,"source_page_last":566,"ar":"وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ مِنَ الصَّالِحاتِ مِنْ ذَكَرٍ أَوْ أُنْثى وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌ فَأُولئِكَ يَدْخُلُونَ الْجَنَّةَ وَلا يُظْلَمُونَ نَقِيراً (124) وَمَنْ أَحْسَنُ دِيناً مِمَّنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ وَاتَّبَعَ مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَاتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ إِبْراهِيمَ خَلِيلاً (125) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُحِيطاً (126)\nقلت: (من ذَكَرٍ أو أنثى) : حال من الضمير في (يعمل) ، وكذا قوله: وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌ و (حنيفًا) ، حال من (إبراهيم) لأنه جزء ما أضيف إليه.\nيقول الحق جلّ جلاله: وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ شيئاً مَنْ الأعمال الصَّالِحاتِ وهو المهم من المكلف به، إذ لا طاقة للبشر على الإتيان بكلها. حال كون العامل مِنْ ذَكَرٍ أَوْ أُنْثى إذ النساء شقائق الرجال في طلب الأعمال، والحالة أن العامل مُؤْمِنٌ لأن الإيمان شرط في قبول الأعمال، فلا ثواب على عمل ليس معه إيمان.\nثم ذكر الجواب فقال: فَأُولئِكَ يَدْخُلُونَ الْجَنَّةَ أي: يتصفون بالدخول، أو يُدخلهم الله الجنة، وَلا يُظْلَمُونَ أي: لا ينقصون من ثواب أعمالهم نَقِيراً أي: مقداره، وهو النقرة في ظهر النواة. قال البيضاوي: وإذا لم ينقص ثواب المطيع فبالأحرى ألا يزيد في عقاب العاصي، لأن المجازي أرحَمُ الراحمين. هـ.\nوَمَنْ أَحْسَنُ دِيناً مِمَّنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ أي: لا أحد أحسن دينًا ممن انقاد بكليته إلى مولاه وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ أي: موحد أحسَنَ فيما بينه وبين الله، وفيما بينه وبين عباد الله، وَاتَّبَعَ مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً بأن دخل في الدين المحمدي الذي هو موافق لملة إبراهيم بل هو عينه، فمن ادعى أنه على ملة إبراهيم ولم يدخل فيه فقد كذب.\nثم ذكر ما يحث على اتباع ملته، فقال: وَاتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ إِبْراهِيمَ خَلِيلًا أي: اصطفاه وخصه بكرامة تشبه كرامة الخليل عند خليله، وإنما أعاد ذكره ولم يضمر تفخيمًا له وتنصيصًا على أنه الممدوح، وسمي خليلاً لأنه قد تخللت محبة الله في جميع أجزائه.\nرُوِي أن إبراهيم عليه السلام كان يضيف الناس، حتى كان يسمى أبا الضيفان، وكان منزله على ظهر الطريق، فأصاب الناسَ سَنَةٌ، جهدوا فيها، فحشد الناسُ إلى باب إبراهيم، يطلبون الطعامَ، وكانت الميرة كل سنة تصله من صديق له بمصر، فبعث غلمانه بالإبل إلى الخليل الذي له بمصر يسأله الميرة، فقال لغلمانه: لو كان إبراهيم يريد لنفسه احتملت له ذلك، ولكنه يريد للأضياف، وقد أصابنا ما أصاب الناس، فرجع الرسل إليه، ومرّوا ببطحاء لينة، فملأوا منها الغرائر حياء من الناس، وأتوا إبراهيم فأخبروه، فاهتم إبراهيم لمكان الناس ببابه، فنام، وكانت سارة نائمة فاستيقظت، وقالت: سبحان الله! أما جاء الغلمان؟ فقالوا: بلى، فقامت إلى الغرائر فإذا فيها أجود الحوّارى- أي: الخالص من الدقيق- فخبزوا وأطعموا؟ فاستيقظ إبراهيم، وشم رائحة الخبز، فقال: يا سارة، من أين هذا؟\nفقالت: من عند خليلك المصري، فقال: هذا من عند خليلي الله- عز وجل-، فحينئذ سماه الله خليلاً (1) .\nقال الزجاج: ومعنى الخليل: الذي ليس في محبته خلل، أو لأنه ردَّ خلَته، أي: فقره إلى الله مخلصًا. هـ.\nوَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ مُلكاً وخلقا وعبيدا، فالملك له، والعبيد عبيده، يختار ما يشاء كما يشاء من خلة ومحبة وخدمة، وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُحِيطاً علمًا وقدرة، فيجازي كلا على قدر سعيه وقصده. والله تعالى أعلم.\nالإشارة: على قدر المجاهدة والمكابدة تكون المعاينة والمشاهدة، على قدر البدايات تكون النهايات، من أشرقت بدايته أشرقت نهايته، والجزاء على العمل يكون على قدر الهمم، فمن عمل لجنة الزخارف مُتع بها، ومن عمل لجنة المعارف تنعم بها، وَلا يَظْلِمُ رَبُّكَ أَحَداً، فمن انقاد بكليته إلى مولاه فلا أحد أحسن منه عند الله، ومن تمسك بالملة الحنيفية، وهي الانقطاع إلى الله بالكلية- فقد استمسك بالعروة الوثقى، وكان في أعلى ذروة أهل التقى، من تخلق بخلق الحبيب كان أقربُ إلى الله من كل قريب. وبالله التوفيق، وهو الهادي إلى سواء الطريق.\nومما يتعلق بحفظ اللسان الفتوى بما يطابق الحق، ولذلك ذكره بعد الأمر بالحكم بالعدل، وما بينهما اعتراض انجرّ الكلام إليه، فقال:","quran_ar":"وَمَنْ يَعْمَلْ مِنَ الصَّالِحاتِ مِنْ ذَكَرٍ أَوْ أُنْثى وَهُوَ مُؤْمِنٌ فَأُولئِكَ يَدْخُلُونَ الْجَنَّةَ وَلا يُظْلَمُونَ نَقِيراً (124) وَمَنْ أَحْسَنُ دِيناً مِمَّنْ أَسْلَمَ وَجْهَهُ لِلَّهِ وَهُوَ مُحْسِنٌ وَاتَّبَعَ مِلَّةَ إِبْراهِيمَ حَنِيفاً وَاتَّخَذَ اللَّهُ إِبْراهِيمَ خَلِيلاً (125) وَلِلَّهِ ما فِي السَّماواتِ وَما فِي الْأَرْضِ وَكانَ اللَّهُ بِكُلِّ شَيْءٍ مُحِيطاً (126)","quran_en":"Whoever does righteous deeds, male or female, being a believer, such people shall enter the Garden and will not be wronged even by the mark on a date-stone. Who is better in religion than one who submits his whole face to God, acts excellently and follows Abraham's upright way? God took Abraham as His intimate friend ( khalīl ). To God belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth; God encompasses all things.","segments":[{"type":"language","title":"","html":"<p>I say: “Male or female” and “being a believer” are circumstantial expressions qualifying the performer; <em>ḥanīfan<\/em> is circumstantial concerning Abraham, since the annexed noun (“way”) is a part of that to which it is annexed (Abraham). Whoever performs some of the righteous deeds—namely, the important duties imposed upon him, since a human being cannot accomplish every such action—whether male or female, enters the promise equally: women are the counterparts of men in works. Faith is the condition for the acceptance of these deeds. Those who act in faith enter Paradise, whether the verb is construed as their doing so or God admitting them. They are deprived of no amount of reward, not even the <em>naqīr<\/em>, the tiny indentation on a date-stone. Al-Bayḍāwī observes that if the obedient do not lose the least of their reward, sinners will still less have their punishment increased unjustly, for the Rewarder is the Most Merciful.<\/p>\n<p>The finest religion belongs to one who yields the whole self to the Lord, practices excellence (<em>iḥsān<\/em>) through divine unity and behaves rightly both toward God and toward His servants, and follows the upright religion of Abraham. In Ibn ʿAjība's presentation this is achieved by entering the Muḥammadan religion, which accords with Abraham’s religion—indeed, is that very religion; anyone who claims to follow Abraham’s religion but has not entered it has, he says, lied. God took Abraham as <em>khalīl<\/em>: He selected him and singled him out for an honour resembling that of an intimate friend with his friend. Repeating Abraham's proper name magnifies him and identifies the one praised. He is called <em>khalīl<\/em> because love of God permeated every part of his being.<\/p>\n<p>Ibn ʿAjība reports that Abraham was known as the father of guests because his home stood beside a road and he fed travellers. In a famine crowds came to his door. Every year a friend in Egypt normally sent him supplies. Abraham sent servants with camels, but the Egyptian friend replied that even he lacked provisions: he would help Abraham personally but could not feed all of Abraham's guests. Ashamed to return with empty sacks, the servants filled them with soft sand as they passed through a valley. Abraham learned their news and worried about his hungry visitors, then slept. Sarah awoke and said, “Glory be to God! Have the servants not arrived?” They replied, “Yes.” She went to the sacks and found in them the purest white flour. They baked bread and fed the guests. Abraham awoke and smelled bread. Told it had come from his Egyptian friend, he answered, “It is from my intimate Friend, God.” It was then, the report says, that God named him His intimate friend. [Cairo editor’s note: citing Ibn Kathīr, the editor questions this story’s authenticity and occurrence. At most, he says, it is an Israelite report, neither affirmed nor denied. Abraham was called God’s intimate friend because of his intense love for God, expressed in obedience that God loves and approves.] Al-Zajjāj explains <em>khalīl<\/em> either as one whose love has no flaw (<em>khalal<\/em>) or one who refers his need (<em>khalla<\/em>) wholly and sincerely to God. Since all heavens and earth are God's created possessions and servants, He chooses whom He wills for love, intimacy and service. His knowledge and power encompass all things and He recompenses every intention and striving.<\/p>\n","authorial":false,"text":"I say: “Male or female” and “being a believer” are circumstantial expressions qualifying the performer; ḥanīfan is circumstantial concerning Abraham, since the annexed noun (“way”) is a part of that to which it is annexed (Abraham). Whoever performs some of the righteous deeds—namely, the important duties imposed upon him, since a human being cannot accomplish every such action—whether male or female, enters the promise equally: women are the counterparts of men in works. Faith is the condition for the acceptance of these deeds. Those who act in faith enter Paradise, whether the verb is construed as their doing so or God admitting them. They are deprived of no amount of reward, not even the naqīr , the tiny indentation on a date-stone. Al-Bayḍāwī observes that if the obedient do not lose the least of their reward, sinners will still less have their punishment increased unjustly, for the Rewarder is the Most Merciful. The finest religion belongs to one who yields the whole self to the Lord, practices excellence ( iḥsān ) through divine unity and behaves rightly both toward God and toward His servants, and follows the upright religion of Abraham. In Ibn ʿAjība's presentation this is achieved by entering the Muḥammadan religion, which accords with Abraham’s religion—indeed, is that very religion; anyone who claims to follow Abraham’s religion but has not entered it has, he says, lied. God took Abraham as khalīl : He selected him and singled him out for an honour resembling that of an intimate friend with his friend. Repeating Abraham's proper name magnifies him and identifies the one praised. He is called khalīl because love of God permeated every part of his being. Ibn ʿAjība reports that Abraham was known as the father of guests because his home stood beside a road and he fed travellers. In a famine crowds came to his door. Every year a friend in Egypt normally sent him supplies. Abraham sent servants with camels, but the Egyptian friend replied that even he lacked provisions: he would help Abraham personally but could not feed all of Abraham's guests. Ashamed to return with empty sacks, the servants filled them with soft sand as they passed through a valley. Abraham learned their news and worried about his hungry visitors, then slept. Sarah awoke and said, “Glory be to God! Have the servants not arrived?” They replied, “Yes.” She went to the sacks and found in them the purest white flour. They baked bread and fed the guests. Abraham awoke and smelled bread. Told it had come from his Egyptian friend, he answered, “It is from my intimate Friend, God.” It was then, the report says, that God named him His intimate friend. [Cairo editor’s note: citing Ibn Kathīr, the editor questions this story’s authenticity and occurrence. At most, he says, it is an Israelite report, neither affirmed nor denied. Abraham was called God’s intimate friend because of his intense love for God, expressed in obedience that God loves and approves.] Al-Zajjāj explains khalīl either as one whose love has no flaw ( khalal ) or one who refers his need ( khalla ) wholly and sincerely to God. Since all heavens and earth are God's created possessions and servants, He chooses whom He wills for love, intimacy and service. His knowledge and power encompass all things and He recompenses every intention and striving."},{"type":"spiritual","title":"","html":"<p>Direct vision corresponds to the measure of struggle and hardship; endings correspond to beginnings. A beginning radiant with sincere aspiration leads to a radiant end. Reward is proportioned to aspiration (<em>himma<\/em>): one who works for a garden of outward delights enjoys it, and one who works for the garden of divine knowledges enjoys that; God wrongs no one. No one is finer than the person entirely surrendered to the Lord. One clinging to Abraham's upright way, understood inwardly as total severance from everything but God, grasps the firmest handle and reaches a summit of reverent godliness. Whoever takes on the Beloved's moral qualities becomes nearer to God than any other who is near. God gives success. Giving a <em>fatwā<\/em> in accord with the truth is one of the matters concerning guarding the tongue. It is therefore mentioned after the command to judge justly; the intervening discussion is a digression into which the discourse was drawn.<\/p>\n","authorial":true,"text":"Direct vision corresponds to the measure of struggle and hardship; endings correspond to beginnings. A beginning radiant with sincere aspiration leads to a radiant end. Reward is proportioned to aspiration ( himma ): one who works for a garden of outward delights enjoys it, and one who works for the garden of divine knowledges enjoys that; God wrongs no one. No one is finer than the person entirely surrendered to the Lord. One clinging to Abraham's upright way, understood inwardly as total severance from everything but God, grasps the firmest handle and reaches a summit of reverent godliness. Whoever takes on the Beloved's moral qualities becomes nearer to God than any other who is near. God gives success. Giving a fatwā in accord with the truth is one of the matters concerning guarding the tongue. It is therefore mentioned after the command to judge justly; the intervening discussion is a digression into which the discourse was drawn."}],"cairo_notes":[{"id":"C566.1","page":566,"ar":"قال ابن كثير: في صحة هذا ووقوعه نظر. وغايته أن يكون خبراً إسرائيلياً، لا يصدق ولا يكذب، وإنما سمي خليل الله لشدة محبته لربه - عز وجل - ما قام له به من الطاعة، التي يحبها ويرضاها.","en":"Ibn Kathīr said: The authenticity of this and its occurrence are questionable. At most it is an Israelite report, neither to be affirmed nor denied. He was called God’s intimate friend only because of his intense love for his Lord—exalted and majestic—and the obedience he rendered Him, which He loves and approves.","anchor":"فحينئذ سماه الله خليلاً"}],"english_characters":4287,"arabic_characters":3506}];
